.SH "NAME"
gitmergeconflicts \- Guide to handling merge conflicts
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
Merge conflicts can happen during a \fBgit\fR \fBmerge\fR, \fBgit\fR \fBrebase\fR, \fBgit\fR \fBcherry\-pick\fR, \fBgit\fR \fBpull\fR, \fBgit\fR \fBrevert\fR, or any other command that does a merge\&. All of those commands use the same merge algorithm, and the process for resolving a merge conflict is always very similar\&.
.sp
There are 3 ways to handle a merge conflict:
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Resolve the conflict\&. (see
HOW TO RESOLVE A MERGE CONFLICT
below for details)
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Stop the operation and return your branch to its original state with the appropriate
\fB\-\-abort\fR
command, for example
\fBgit\fR
\fBmerge\fR
\fB\-\-abort\fR
or
\fBgit\fR
\fBrebase\fR
\fB\-\-abort\fR\&. See
EXAMPLE: GIT STATUS OUTPUT
below for how to find the command to run\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Or (if you\(cqre in a multi\-step operation like a rebase) skip the problematic commit with the appropriate
\fB\-\-skip\fR
command\&.
.RE
.SH "WHAT IS A MERGE CONFLICT?"
.sp
When Git merges two commits together, it looks at the changes that each side has made and combines those changes\&. For example, if one side edited lines 1\-5 of \fBhello\&.py\fR and the other side edited lines 20\-25 of the same file, then it can easily combine them since there\(cqs no overlap\&.
.sp
But if both sides edited overlapping lines of the same file (for example one side edited lines 1\-5 and the other edited lines 3\-6), Git will not try to guess how to combine those changes\&. This is called a "merge conflict"\&.
.sp
When this happens, Git shows you both sides\*(Aq edits and asks you to pick how to resolve them\&. It:
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Stages all of the files which were successfully merged
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
For the files with conflicts, it marks them as conflicted, puts both sides\*(Aq edits in the file, and leaves
merge conflict markers
that you need to resolve\&.
.RE
.if n \{\
.sp
.\}
.RS 4
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.ps +1
\fBNote\fR
.ps -1
.br
.sp
Even if Git successfully merges both sides with no conflicts, it\(cqs possible to end up with code that doesn\(cqt work\&. For example, this can happen if one side renames a function, and the other side adds a call to the old function\&. This is called a "semantic merge conflict"\&.
.sp .5v
.RE
.SH "MERGE CONFLICT MARKERS"
.sp
When there\(cqs a merge conflict, Git will update the conflicted file to include merge conflict markers <<<<<<<, \fB=======\fR, and >>>>>>>\&. For example, here\(cqs a merge conflict where both sides edited a list of fruits in different ways \(em one side has "cherry" and the other side has "banana":
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
FRUITS = [
    "apple",
<<<<<<< HEAD
    "cherry",
=======
    "banana",
>>>>>>> add\-fruit
    "mango",
    "orange",
]
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
The lines from one side of the merge conflict is between <<<<<<< and \fB=======\fR, and the lines from the other side is between \fB=======\fR and >>>>>>>\&. See "OURS" AND "THEIRS" below for a full explanation of which side is which\&.
.SH "HOW TO RESOLVE A MERGE CONFLICT"
.sp
The process for resolving a merge conflict is:
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' 1.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP "  1." 4.2
.\}
Run
\fBgit\fR
\fBstatus\fR
to get a list of files with merge conflicts\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' 2.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP "  2." 4.2
.\}
For each one, find the conflict markers (the <<<<<<<,
\fB=======\fR, >>>>>>>) and edit the file to fix the conflict\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' 3.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP "  3." 4.2
.\}
Run
\fBgit\fR
\fBadd\fR
\fBFILENAME\fR
for each file to mark the conflict as resolved\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' 4.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP "  4." 4.2
.\}
Run the appropriate
\fB\-\-continue\fR
command to continue the operation that was interrupted by the conflict, for example
\fBgit\fR
\fBmerge\fR
\fB\-\-continue\fR
or
\fBgit\fR
\fBrebase\fR
\fB\-\-continue\fR\&. See
EXAMPLE: GIT STATUS OUTPUT
below for how to find the command to run\&.
.sp
Note: During a
\fBgit\fR
\fBmerge\fR,
\fBgit\fR
\fBcommit\fR
and
\fBgit\fR
\fBmerge\fR
\fB\-\-continue\fR
do the same thing\&.
.RE
.SH "EXAMPLE OF RESOLVING A MERGE CONFLICT"
.sp
If you see this in your code during a merge conflict:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
FRUITS = [
    "apple",
<<<<<<< HEAD
    "cherry",
=======
    "banana",
>>>>>>> add\-fruit
    "mango",
    "orange",
]
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
Then you might edit that part of the code like this, which includes the fruits from both sides of the conflict:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
FRUITS = [
    "apple",
    "banana",
    "cherry",
    "mango",
    "orange",
]
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.SH "TOOLS FOR HANDLING MERGE CONFLICTS"
.sp
Here are some ways to get extra context while handling a merge conflict:
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
There are many graphical "merge tools" for Git, which will normally show you the different versions of the code side by side\&. If you have a mergetool configured,
\fBgit\fR
\fBmergetool\fR
will launch it\&. See also
\fBmerge\&.tool\fR
in
\fBgit-config\fR(1)
for a list of the mergetools Git supports\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
You can set the configuration variable
\fBmerge\&.conflictstyle=diff3\fR\&. See
DIFF3
below for more\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\fBgit\fR
\fBlog\fR
\fB\-\-merge\fR
\fB\-\-patch\fR
\fI<filename>\fR
will list all commits which caused the merge conflict for
\fI<filename>\fR, and the diff of how they changed the file\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Look at the original files\&.
\fBgit\fR
\fBshow\fR
\fB:1:filename\fR
shows the common ancestor,
\fBgit\fR
\fBshow\fR
\fB:2:filename\fR
shows the "ours" version, and
\fBgit\fR
\fBshow\fR
\fB:3:filename\fR
shows the "theirs" version\&. See
"OURS" AND "THEIRS"
below for more on "ours" and "theirs"\&.
.RE
.sp
Here are some ways to track your progress while handling a conflict:
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Use
\fBgit\fR
\fBstatus\fR
to get a list of files with conflicts
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Use
\fBgit\fR
\fBdiff\fR
\fB\-\-check\fR
to make sure you haven\(cqt left any merge conflict markers in a file by accident\&. It will print "leftover conflict marker" if it finds any\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Use
\fBgit\fR
\fBdiff\fR
\fBAUTO_MERGE\fR
to show what changes you\(cqve made so far to resolve the conflicts\&. See
EXAMPLE OF USING \fBAUTO_MERGE\fR
below for more\&.
.RE
.SH "EXAMPLE: GIT STATUS OUTPUT"
.sp
When you\(cqre in a merge conflict, you can find out what commands to run to handle the conflict by running \fBgit\fR \fBstatus\fR\&.
.sp
For example, this \fBgit\fR \fBstatus\fR output tells you that:
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\fBgit\fR
\fBrebase\fR
\fB\-\-abort\fR
will safely bring your branch back to its original state
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
you should run
\fBgit\fR
\fBrebase\fR
\fB\-\-continue\fR
when you\(cqre done resolving all the conflicts
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
the conflicts in
\fBfruits\&.py\fR
haven\(cqt been marked as resolved
.RE
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
$ git status
You are currently rebasing branch \*(Aqmain\*(Aq on \*(Aq58a9fcc\*(Aq\&.
  (fix conflicts and then run "git rebase \-\-continue")
  (use "git rebase \-\-skip" to skip this patch)
  (use "git rebase \-\-abort" to check out the original branch)

Unmerged paths:
  (use "git restore \-\-staged <file>\&.\&.\&." to unstage)
  (use "git add <file>\&.\&.\&." to mark resolution)
        both modified:   fruits\&.py
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.SH "DIFF3"
.sp
By default, Git doesn\(cqt include the original code when formatting a merge conflict\&. To include the original code, you can set the configuration option \fBmerge\&.conflictstyle\fR to \fBdiff3\fR\&. This extra context can make it much easier to understand what\(cqs happening in a merge conflict\&.
.sp
Here\(cqs an example of what a merge conflict would look like when using \fBdiff3\fR\&. There\(cqs an extra section (between ||||||| and \fB=======\fR) with the original code\&.
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
FRUITS = [
    "apple",
<<<<<<< HEAD
    "cherry",
    "mango",
||||||| 1c22e48
    "banana",
    "cherry",
    "mangoooo",
=======
    "banana",
    "mango",
>>>>>>> add\-fruit
    "orange",
]
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
With this view, you can see that both sides fixed a spelling mistake in "mango", and each removed one fruit from the list\&. This makes it more clear how to resolve the merge conflict: since one side removed \fBbanana\fR and one side removed \fBcherry\fR, it would probably make sense to remove both from the list, like this:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
FRUITS = [
    "apple",
    "mango",
    "orange",
]
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.if n \{\
.sp
.\}
.RS 4
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.ps +1
\fBNote\fR
.ps -1
.br
.sp
\fBzdiff3\fR is an alternative to \fBdiff3\fR that makes the merge conflict shorter by making it more ambiguous\&. See \fBgit-config\fR(1) for more\&.
.sp .5v
.RE
.SH ""OURS" AND "THEIRS""
.sp
Sometimes during a merge conflict, Git will use the terms "ours" and "theirs" (or "us" and "them")\&. For example, \fBgit\fR \fBstatus\fR might say that a file was \fBdeleted\fR \fBby\fR \fBus\fR\&.
.sp
"Ours" and "theirs" are both commits: "ours" is the current \fBHEAD\fR commit, and "theirs" is the other side being merged\&.
.sp
The first part of a merge conflict (between <<<<<<< and \fB=======\fR) is from the "ours" side, and the second part (between \fB=======\fR and >>>>>>>) is from the "theirs" side\&.
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
FRUITS = [
    "apple",
<<<<<<< HEAD
    "cherry",                      <\- ours
=======
    "banana",                      <\- theirs
>>>>>>> add\-fruit
    "mango",
    "orange",
]
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
During a rebase, it can seem "upside down" because the "ours" commit is from the branch you\(cqre rebasing on (for instance \fBmain\fR in \fBgit\fR \fBrebase\fR \fBmain\fR)\&.
.sp
These terms in Git all mean the same thing when dealing with a merge conflict:
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
"common ancestor" and "base"\&. The files from this commit are "in stage 1"\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
"ours", "us", and
\fBHEAD\fR\&. The files from this commit are "in stage 2"\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
"theirs", "them"\&. The files from this commit are "in stage 3"\&.
.RE
.sp
If you\(cqre confused about what something like "deleted by us" means, it\(cqs often easiest to use some of the tools from TOOLS FOR HANDLING MERGE CONFLICTS above to get more context\&. Finding the commit that deleted the file and seeing why is usually more helpful than trying to abstractly reason through what "us" means\&.
.SH "EXAMPLE OF USING \FBAUTO_MERGE\FR"
.sp
\fBgit\fR \fBdiff\fR \fBAUTO_MERGE\fR will show what changes you\(cqve made so far to resolve conflicts\&. \fBAUTO_MERGE\fR is a reference that Git creates during a merge\&. It contains the result of running the merge algorithm\&.
.sp
For example, if we resolved the conflict by adding both "banana" and "cherry" in order, the diff would look like this:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
 FRUITS = [
     "apple",
\-<<<<<<< HEAD
\-    "cherry",
\-=======
     "banana",
\->>>>>>> add\-fruit
+    "cherry",
     "mango",
     "orange",
 ]
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.if n \{\
.sp
.\}
.RS 4
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.ps +1
\fBNote\fR
.ps -1
.br
.sp
\fBAUTO_MERGE\fR is only set if you\(cqre using the default Git merge algorithm\&.
.sp .5v
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.sp
